From: SORABBYNGO Engineering <kernel@sorabbyngo.com>
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] kaslr: improve entropy mixing — OpenBSD-inspired boot-time randomisation

OpenBSD uses a combination of CPU RDRAND, TSC jitter, and boot-time
memory layout randomisation. This patch strengthens the existing Linux
kASLR with:

  1. Additional TSC jitter mixed into the ASLR seed at early boot
  2. PIE binary randomisation for all setuid executables (matching
     OpenBSD's default-PIE policy)
  3. Randomised mmap base per-process (already in Linux, but we extend
     the entropy to 28 bits on x86_64 — matching OpenBSD's 32-bit range)
  4. Stack gap randomisation extended from 8 bits to 16 bits

These are low-risk changes — they do not alter kernel ABI, only the
entropy of address layout decisions.
---
 arch/x86/boot/compressed/kaslr.c |  24 ++++++
 mm/util.c                        |  12 +++
 fs/binfmt_elf.c                  |   8 ++
 3 files changed, 44 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/boot/compressed/kaslr.c b/arch/x86/boot/compressed/kaslr.c
--- a/arch/x86/boot/compressed/kaslr.c
+++ b/arch/x86/boot/compressed/kaslr.c
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 #include "misc.h"
 #include "error.h"
+#include <asm/msr.h>
 
+/*
+ * sorabbyngo_mix_tsc_entropy - mix TSC jitter into the KASLR seed.
+ *
+ * OpenBSD does this in locore.S by reading RDTSC multiple times with
+ * deliberate memory barriers to accumulate jitter from branch prediction
+ * and cache effects. We replicate the intent in C.
+ */
+static unsigned long sorabbyngo_mix_tsc_entropy(unsigned long seed)
+{
+	unsigned long tsc1, tsc2, tsc3;
+	unsigned int lo, hi;
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
+		rdtsc_barrier();
+		rdtsc(lo, hi);
+		tsc1 = ((unsigned long)hi << 32) | lo;
+		/* deliberate dependent load to induce jitter */
+		asm volatile("lfence" ::: "memory");
+		rdtsc(lo, hi);
+		tsc2 = ((unsigned long)hi << 32) | lo;
+		tsc3 = tsc2 - tsc1;
+		/* fold low bits (highest jitter) into seed */
+		seed ^= (tsc3 & 0xff) << (i & 0x3f);
+		seed  = (seed << 1) | (seed >> 63);
+	}
+	return seed;
+}
